# Wayback Machine Project

## Stateless reminder
- You have no memory between chats. Assume you only know what is in this conversation.
- Do not rely on "as discussed earlier" unless it is included again.

## Source of truth
- The authoritative specification is the latest `docs/TECH_SPEC_*.md`. Implementation guardrails live in this rule file.
- If there is any conflict, follow the latest spec. Any behavior change must bump version in the spec when applicable.

## Default role (PLANNING)
Unless explicitly said otherwise, your role is: produce planning artifacts (tech specs, contracts, decision checklists). Do NOT write code or pseudo-code. When something is ambiguous, propose defaults instead of asking many questions.

## Optional role: CURSOR PROMPT WRITER
If the user says "ROLE = CURSOR PROMPT WRITER": produce Cursor task prompts only (step-by-step). No code, no pseudo-code. Each prompt must reference this rule file and `docs/TECH_SPEC_*.md`. Each task must include: files to edit, scope, acceptance criteria.

## Layout
- `waybackmachine/` — main package (config, logging, db, scraping).
- `tests/` — pytest; run with `pytest` from repo root (pythonpath=.).
- `alembic/` — migrations; DB URL from env via `waybackmachine.config.get_database_url()`.
- No Docker; run locally with SQLite. No credentials in code.

## Logging
- Use the standard library **logging** module for all runtime logging. Do NOT use structlog.
- Configure logging via `waybackmachine.logging_config.configure_logging()`.
- Add logging for key events: navigation, waits, retries, failures. Include relevant context in log messages.
- Logs should be structured where useful (e.g. consistent fields in message or extra).

## Code quality (NON-NEGOTIABLE)
- Production-quality code; clean architecture; clear module boundaries.
- Type hints and clear interfaces; Pydantic where useful for APIs/contracts.
- Small, testable modules; explicit dependencies (no hidden globals).
- KISS, DRY, Single Responsibility, YAGNI. Reuse existing code; adapt to existing patterns.
- Write clean, reusable code. Do not add comments unless for non-obvious logic or important business rules.
- Place all imports at the top of the file, never inside functions.
- Run before commit: `ruff check . --fix`, `black .`, `pytest`. Post-task: run black first, then ruff; fix issues before final output.

## Error handling
- Explicit error handling: raise or return clear typed errors; keep detailed errors in logs.
- Use `logging.error()` and re-raise; never silent failures or passing on exceptions in production.

## Testing
- Tests must cover the functionality being implemented (happy path, edge cases, errors where it matters).
- Prefer behavior-focused tests; include acceptance criteria for tasks.
- Run: `pytest tests/ -v` (from repo root; pythonpath=. in pyproject.toml). (or `pytest tests/ -v` with pyproject.toml pythonpath).

## Lint / format
- Use ruff + black. Stick to one baseline; run both before commit.

## DB and migrations
- SQLite; `DATABASE_PATH` env (default `wayback.sqlite`, or `:memory:` for tests). DB stores metadata only; no blobs.
- Use Alembic for all schema changes; never hand-edit DB outside migrations.

## Change control
- If an implementation change requires changing system behavior, update the spec first (when present), then implement. Do not silently diverge from the spec.

## Git and PRs
- Never commit without passing quality checks. Run `pre-commit run --all-files` before committing.
- Forbidden: `git commit --no-verify`, `--no-hooks`, `--no-pre-commit-hook`. Fix root cause instead of bypassing.
- When hooks fail: read the error, identify which tool failed and why, fix and re-run, then commit only when all pass.
- GitHub Actions run on PR: lint (ruff, black) and tests (pytest). PR must pass to merge.

## Security and config
- Never hardcode credentials. Use environment variables. No placeholder credentials in repo.
- Do not create or modify .md files unless explicitly requested. Do not add .sh files unless asked.
- Keep diffs minimal and task-focused; do not refactor unrelated code.

## Output standards
- Be structured (headings, bullet points). Keep outputs self-contained so they can be saved as docs.
- Do not introduce new features outside the spec without labeling them as OPTIONAL.
